  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite reveals an organized cluster of\nstrong to severe thunderstorms moving southeast along a narrow\ninstability gradient across portions of Northwest and North- \nCentral Ohio early this morning, supported by modest northwest \nshear of 30 to 35 knots. Damaging wind gusts up to 60 mph remain\nthe primary concern with this area of convection as it exits \nsouth and east of the area by around sunrise. A Severe \nThunderstorm Watch remains in effect for this area until 8 AM. \n\nOtherwise, a cold front will move south through the area today,\nushering in a cooler and less humid air mass into the region.\nThis could result in some scattered showers or isolated \nthunderstorms, though strong to severe storms are not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will begin to build south across the Great Lakes \nlate Saturday and persist into early next week. The latest \ntrends favor seasonable and mainly dry weather during this \ntimeframe with highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s and less \nhumidity. \n\nWill then begin watching the next system by mid-next week as\nanother upper-level ridge builds east across the Central and\nEastern CONUS, followed by the passage of a strong upper-level\ntrough moving east through the Upper Great Lakes. Shower and\nthunderstorms chances will increase beginning Wednesday \nafternoon and evening, with the potential continuing into\nThursday, though uncertainty remains on storm coverage.",
